1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:03,214
What stops one admin from switching on a fraudulent shop?

2
00:00:03,214 --> 00:00:07,488
If one person can do it, one mistake or one bribe is enough.

3
00:00:07,488 --> 00:00:12,940
So activation becomes a request: a maker creates it, and nothing changes yet.

4
00:00:12,940 --> 00:00:18,595
A different admin, the checker, approves it, and only then does the merchant become active.

5
00:00:18,595 --> 00:00:21,166
The maker can never approve their own request.

6
00:00:21,166 --> 00:00:25,988
A request that waits too long expires after the configured time.

7
00:00:25,988 --> 00:00:31,940
A scheduled sweep marks old requests as expired, and a lock makes sure it runs only once.

8
00:00:31,940 --> 00:00:38,464
Only one pending request per merchant can exist, enforced by a unique index in the database.

9
00:00:38,464 --> 00:00:43,809
Next, logins and API keys, without storing a single password ourselves.

